Chapter: SpikeProfileMain
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Poc chapter Spike

 SPIKE ONLY. THROWAWAY. The speed profile: the ported physics over the whole
 route, one point per frame, as a single line for harness/spike_svg.py to draw and
 for `harness/metal.py --entry` to diff against the other arm.

 AN ENTRY OF ITS OWN BECAUSE ONE LINE CAN COST MORE THAN A FRAME. `prof-all`
 concatenates right-recursively, so building a 4,000-point line allocates the sum
 of its own prefixes -- quadratic -- and the bump heap never gives any of it back
 (PORTING_NOTES C6). At the old scaled-integer width that was about 160 MB and it
 rode along in SpikeMain unnoticed. Bit patterns are roughly twice the characters,
 which makes it 640 MB, and beside two frames it exhausted the 4 GiB reserve
 outright. Splitting is the same answer C6 always has; the pleasant side effect is
 that every other spike entry is now exactly two viewpoints.

 IT IS NOT DRAWN FROM A CLOCK. `speed-profile` is `profile-from` over the real
 rider fold, so this is the same physics `judge/RideCheck.codex` grades by
 invariants -- one variable, route distance, and no animation anywhere.

 We say:

Section: Entry

 dist, v and tilt as IEEE-754 patterns; `seg` is already an Integer and is printed
 as itself. `Poc chapter SpikePrint` is not cited: it exists to share the DRAW
 COMMAND format between the frame entries, and a profile point is not a draw
 command.

  prof-pt : ProfilePt -> Text
  prof-pt (p) =
    show (real-to-bits p.dist) & " " & show (real-to-bits p.v) & " " & show (real-to-bits p.tilt) & " " & show p.seg

  prof-all : List ProfilePt, Integer -> Text
  prof-all (ps) (i) = prof-range ps i (list-length ps)

  prof-range : List ProfilePt, Integer, Integer -> Text
  prof-range (ps) (lo) (hi) =
    if hi <= lo then ""
    else if hi - lo == 1 then prof-pt (list-at ps lo) & " ; "
    else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
      in prof-range ps lo mid & prof-range ps mid hi

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("PROFILE " & show (list-length speed-profile))
    print-line-uni (prof-all speed-profile 0)
  end
